<DocsLayout title="Advanced Pydantic AI guide | dotfetch" description="Approvals, tool search, skills, toolset ids, tracing and error modes for the dotfetch Pydantic AI integration.">
  <p class="eyebrow">Advanced framework guide</p>
  <h1>Advanced Pydantic AI</h1>
  <p class="lead">Human-in-the-loop approval, dotfetch-backed tool search, per-turn skills, multiple toolsets, tracing, and how per-turn selection behaves inside a run.</p>
  <p><a class="pill" href="/Agent-Gantry/docs/frameworks/pydantic-ai/">← Basic Pydantic AI usage</a></p>

  <h2>Human-in-the-loop approval</h2>
  <p>A tool registered with <code>requires_confirmation=True</code>, or matched by a <code>SecurityPolicy(require_confirmation=[...])</code> pattern, pauses the run with Pydantic AI's <code>DeferredToolRequests</code>. Approving it with <code>DeferredToolResults</code> replays the call once through <code>fetcher.execute</code> with the confirmation gate cleared; every other policy check still runs. A denial never executes the tool.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`from pydantic_ai import Agent, DeferredToolRequests, DeferredToolResults

agent = Agent(
    "openai:gpt-5.5",
    output_type=[str, DeferredToolRequests],   # required for approval-gated tools
    capabilities=[adapter.capability()],
)

result = await agent.run("Refund order 4471")
if isinstance(result.output, DeferredToolRequests):
    decisions = {call.tool_call_id: ask_a_human(call) for call in result.output.approvals}
    result = await agent.run(
        message_history=result.all_messages(),
        deferred_tool_results=DeferredToolResults(approvals=decisions),
    )`} />
  <p>Each request's metadata carries dotfetch's identity for the tool under <code>"dotfetch"</code> (its qualified name and the reason it needs approval).</p>

  <h2>Tool search backed by dotfetch</h2>
  <p><code>capability(mode="search")</code> combines a deferred catalogue (every dotfetch tool marked <code>defer_loading</code>) with Pydantic AI's <code>ToolSearch</code>, using dotfetch's semantic router as the search strategy. The model calls <code>search_tools</code>, or the provider's native tool search where supported, and only discovered tools reach it. Tools passed as <code>required</code> or <code>always_include</code> stay visible.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`agent = Agent(model, capabilities=[adapter.capability(mode="search", limit=5)])

# Or compose the parts yourself:
agent = Agent(
    model,
    toolsets=[adapter.deferred_toolset(always_include=["get_order_status"])],
    capabilities=[adapter.tool_search(limit=5)],
)`} />
  <p>Other deferred tools on the same agent (not managed by dotfetch) are ranked by keyword and merged with dotfetch's semantic ranking under the same <code>limit</code>, so an exact keyword match is not crowded out by an unrelated dotfetch hit.</p>

  <h2>Per-turn selection inside a run</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Query:</strong> the newest user prompt; after a tool call, the run's prompt plus the newest tool result (first 2,000 characters). Tools the model already called feed the router's <code>tools_already_used</code> signal.</li>
    <li><strong>Cost:</strong> selections are cached per run, so there is one retrieval per distinct query, not one per step.</li>
    <li><strong>Privacy:</strong> tool-result text in the query is sent to the embedder, which is a remote service for OpenAI-style embedders. Pass <code>query_from_results=False</code> to <code>toolset()</code> or <code>capability()</code> to keep tool output out of retrieval.</li>
    <li><strong>Stickiness:</strong> with <code>sticky=True</code> (the default), tools offered earlier in a run stay offered, so a plan the model made at step one doesn't lose a tool at step three. <code>max_offered</code> caps how many are offered per step (default <code>3 * limit</code>): the current selection and pinned tools always stay, and older tools are dropped least recently selected first. Use <code>sticky=False</code> for a strict top-k.</li>
    <li><strong>Concurrency:</strong> run state lives on the per-run copy Pydantic AI's <code>for_run</code> hook creates, so concurrent runs can share one toolset safely.</li>
    <li><strong>Failure:</strong> if retrieval fails mid-run, the step keeps the run's earlier tools and logs a warning; the run continues.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Several dotfetch toolsets on one agent</h2>
  <p>Give each one a distinct <code>id</code> (durable execution keys on it) and avoid name clashes with a namespace filter or a prefix.</p>
  <CodeBlock lang="python" code={`agent = Agent(
    model,
    toolsets=[
        adapter.toolset(id="billing", namespaces=["billing"]),
        adapter.toolset(id="support", namespaces=["support"]).prefixed("support"),
    ],
)`} />

  <h2>Skills as instructions</h2>
  <p><code>capability(skills=2)</code> injects up to two dotfetch skills relevant to the current turn as dynamic instructions. Restrict them with <code>skill_namespaces=[...]</code>. If skill retrieval fails, the step continues without them.</p>

  <h2>Tracing</h2>
  <p>When Pydantic AI is instrumented (Logfire or any OpenTelemetry backend), the active tool span's ids become the dotfetch <code>ToolCall</code>'s <code>trace_id</code> and <code>parent_span_id</code>, so <code>fetcher.on_tool_call</code> events and dotfetch's own telemetry join the agent's trace.</p>

  <h2>Production checklist</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Add <code>DeferredToolRequests</code> to <code>output_type</code> on any agent that can reach a confirmation-gated tool.</li>
    <li>Use search mode for large catalogues or prompt-cache-sensitive agents; per-turn mode for small, fast-moving tool sets.</li>
    <li>Read <code>deps</code> through <code>current_run_context()</code>. It is not available to out-of-process executors (sandbox, MCP, A2A, HTTP).</li>
    <li>Keep <code>error_mode="model"</code> unless your application handles <code>ToolExecutionError</code> itself. To keep internals (paths, hostnames, connection strings) out of what the model sees, pass <code>PydanticAIAdapter(fetcher, error_formatter=redact)</code>; a formatter that raises is replaced by a generic message.</li>
  </ul>
